A||N||4||carfakes||problem starting when hot||||5||
Z||000000||carfakes||10-25-2000||11:54 PM||||I am looking for anyone who may have an answer to a problem I am having.  I bought an 87 se 2.8 a week ago.  The car looks great and drives fine, but when it shuts off it takes about 5-10 mins until I can start it again.  Until then it acts as though the battery is dead and just barely turns over the engine but not fast enough to start.  If I wait 10 mins it then fires up like a brand new car.  I have a new battery and new plugs, wires, and distributer so I know it is not related to that.  If anyone knows how to fix this, please e-mail me at carfakes@yahoo.com||||reg||5
Z||000001||RossT||10-26-2000||12:00 AM||||GM has a TSB about this. I was just reading about it in a back issue of FOCOA. I will try and find it when I get home from work, but try to do a search "hot start".  We have talked about this in the past.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||10-26-2000||04:40 AM||||Replace the starter.<P>Starter solenoid is being affected by the heat. V6's don't usually have problems enough to need the Ford solenoid since the exhaust doesn't come as close as it does in a four cyl.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Eric_PA||10-26-2000||09:38 AM||||I had the same problem. Replaced the starter and no more waiting 20 minutes or so before restarting the car. I heard that this was common on some starters due to the heat exposure.<P>Eric<BR>86.5 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-26-2000||07:37 PM||||this is why they make heat shields for the starters. You can get these premade or make it yourself from some stiff aluminum or thin sheet steel.<P>Even if you are replacing the starter, add the shield. the new starter will last longer.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-26-2000).]||||reg||1
